// Magnitudes: plain quantities, tolerances, ranges and alternative-value sets.
//
// Runs on text that has already been through canonicalize(), so by this point the multiplication
// sign is "x", the degree sign is "degC", "±" is "+/-", en dashes are " .. " and the decimal
// separator has been resolved or refused.
//
// Accepted:
//   plain      63 A | 10.0 mm | 230 V AC | max 63 A | 63 A max | <= 40 degC | 12 | 45 %
//   tolerance  10 +/- 0.2 mm | 10 mm +/- 0.2 | 10 mm +/-2% | 10 +0.2/-0.1 mm | 10 +0.3 -0.1 mm
//   range      -25 .. 70 degC | 10 .. 16 A | 10-16 A | 0.5 .. 1.5 Nm
//   set        230/400 V | 400/690 V | 110/230/400 V
//   fraction   1/2 in | 3/8 in | 1 1/2 in
//
// The fraction-versus-set decision is deliberately NOT made here. "3/8" and "230/400" have the
// same shape, and only the magnitudes tell them apart. The grammar emits the digits; the
// transformer decides, and records which reading it took.

?start: dimension

?dimension: rangev
          | tolv
          | plainv
          | bare

// Rule priorities resolve the genuine ambiguities. A reading that consumes a unit beats a reading
// that treats the same token as a trailing qualifier: "10 min" is ten minutes, not ten-with-a-
// minimum-qualifier. That is why `plainv` (which requires a unit) and `bare` (which forbids one)
// are separate rules rather than one rule with an optional unit -- Lark carries priority per rule,
// so the preference has to be expressed in the rule names.
rangev.4: signed unit? RANGESEP signed unit?
        | NUM DASH NUM unit?

tolv.5: signed unit? TOL unit? QUAL?

plainv.3: QUAL? CMP? signed unit QUAL?
bare.1:   QUAL? CMP? signed QUAL?

signed: SIGN? magnitude
magnitude: SLASHNUMS
         | MIXED
         | NUM

unit: UNITTOK

// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- terminals ----
//
// UNITTOK outranks QUAL, and the ordering is load-bearing. "min" is both a qualifier and the
// symbol for a minute; "10 min" is ten minutes and "63 A max" is a bounded current. Where both
// readings complete, the unit reading wins on terminal priority. Where only the qualifier reading
// completes -- "230 V AC" cannot carry two units -- Earley falls back to QUAL on its own. Giving
// QUAL the higher priority instead turns every duration field into a qualified dimensionless
// number, silently.
TOL.9:       /\+\s*\d+(?:\.\d+)?\s*%?\s*\/?\s*-\s*\d+(?:\.\d+)?\s*%?|\+\/-\s*\d+(?:\.\d+)?\s*%?/
UNITTOK.8:   /[A-Za-zΩ%'"][A-Za-z0-9_%*\/^.]*/
QUAL.7:      /(?:AC\/DC|AC|DC|rms|RMS|peak|pk|p-p|pp|max|min|typ|nom|approx|eff|abs)(?![A-Za-z])/
RANGESEP.7:  ".."
CMP.7:       /<=|>=|<|>/
MIXED.6:     /\d+\s+\d+\/\d+(?![\/\d])/
SLASHNUMS.5: /\d+(?:\.\d+)?(?:\/\d+(?:\.\d+)?)+(?![\/\d])/
NUM.4:       /\d+(?:\.\d+)?/
SIGN.3:      /[+-]/
DASH.2:      "-"

%import common.WS
%ignore WS
